title: Forks of foreign data require two more relationships.
pid: 7698f204-7be7-478e-93b3-f88ffab7159c
body: |2-
  The two relations are called 'forks' and 'hides' (as in "hides from its forks").

  When I "edit" a node of yours, I don't change anything for you. I instead create a fork of your node. That fork is a node very much like yours: it has the same text and the same content, except to the extent that I edited those.

  But the fork is created with a new relationship: the fork 'forks' your node. That means two things. First, whenever I view a note of mine involving your node, I will in place of your node see my fork of it. Second, if you add content to your node, it will be visible to me, under my fork.

  (A node can fork multiple other nodes, but let's ignore that for now.)

  If I delete a node from your node's content, that creates a 'hides' relationship from my fork to the deleted node, unless I keep it in another node of mine. Under my fork, I see only the things your node contains that my node neither contains nor hides, and that no other node of mine contains.
